cartridge "VORTEX/S.D.WAGLE/1987"

' VORTEX
' A ColecoVision game based on "Vortex" by Sean D. Wagle, Ahoy! January
' 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language, VORTEX at $C000, started by the
' two-line VORTEX BOOT). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/vortex.dis), the game is rebuilt for the
' ColecoVision: an armoured core of hatched blocks round a glowing eye,
' a small arrow ship drawn at 32 headings, six spinning guardians, a
' starfield, smooth sprites, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' The game: a core sits in the middle of space behind 36 armour blocks.
' Your ship never stops flying; turn it and shoot your way through the
' armour to the eye. Guardians come out of the core and wheel round it;
' touch one, or fly into the armour, and the ship is lost (and the core
' is rebuilt). Shoot the eye to clear the level.
'
' From the original (orig/): the core's shape (7 rows: 3, 5, 7, 7, 7, 5, 3
' blocks with the eye in the middle); the ship always moving the way it
' faces, turned by the stick through 32 headings, wrapping round the
' screen; one shot at a time; NOVICE (every hit breaks a block) and
' EXPERT (a hit breaks a block one time in four); six guardians, each
' born at the core with a random heading and a random speed below the
' level (0-3 then 0-7 from level 7, plus one), turning one heading every
' 10 frames three times in four, so they wheel round the core, and born
' again when they leave the screen; a shot that hits a guardian kills it;
' a guardian touching the ship, or the ship touching the armour, loses a
' ship and rebuilds the core; 3 ships; 100 points a block (the eye too),
' 10 a guardian, a bonus of 1000 x level (2000 x level on EXPERT) for the
' eye; 18 levels, then level 1 again; a new colour for each level.
'
' Liberties: speeds in ColecoVision pixels with the same proportions for
' both axes (the C64's horizontal steps were double its vertical ones):
' ship 1.25 pixels a frame, shot 5, guardians 0.5 x (speed + 1); the ship
' turns one heading every 2 frames (the C64 turned every frame: a whole
' turn in half a second); a press fires and holding a button brakes (the
' C64 fired again while the button was held and braked only while a shot
' was out, so holding the button made a sitting gun); the ship waits at
' the start of each life until the stick or a button is touched; it
' starts at the bottom left facing right (the C64 started it facing the
' core); from level 2 a guardian sometimes turns toward the ship instead
' of wheeling (2 x level chances in 64 at each turn) so a ship cannot sit
' safely in one place; the score has six digits and stops at 999999; a
' best score; GAME OVER then the title.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): left and right turn the ship, a button
' fires, holding a button brakes. On the title, up and down choose,
' a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Own xorshift random numbers.

